Shared Memory, Memory-Mapped Files, Semaphores and Mutexes


Introduction:
Interprocess Communication (IPC) allows different processes to communicate and share data with each other.
Shared memory and memory-mapped files are used for sharing data, while semaphores and mutexes are mainly used for synchronization and controlling access to shared resources.


1. Shared Memory:
 Shared memory is an IPC method where two or more processes access the same area of memory.

Working
  A common memory area is created.
  Multiple processes can access it.
  One process can write data and another can read it.

Advantages
  Fast communication.
  Efficient for large amounts of data.
  Reduces unnecessary data copying.

Python: multiprocessing.shared_memory, Value, and Array can be used.

2. Memory-Mapped Files:
 A memory-mapped file maps a file or part of a file into memory. Processes can then access the file data through memory.

Advantages
  Faster and convenient access to file data.
  Useful for large files.
  Shared mappings can allow multiple processes to access common data.
  
Python: The mmap module is used.

3. Semaphores:
 A semaphore is a synchronization technique that controls how many processes or threads can access a shared resource at the same time.

Working
  A process acquires the semaphore before accessing the resource.
  It uses the resource.
  It releases the semaphore after completion.

Python: multiprocessing.Semaphore() is used.

4.  Mutexes:
 A mutex (Mutual Exclusion) allows only one process or thread at a time to enter a protected critical section.

Working
  A process locks the mutex.
  It accesses the shared resource.
  It unlocks the mutex when finished.
  Other processes wait until the lock is released.

Python: multiprocessing.Lock() can provide mutual exclusion.

Semaphore vs Mutex:
Semaphore                                         Mutex
Uses a counter/permits.                           Uses mutual exclusion.
Can allow multiple users depending on the count.  Allows only one user in the protected section at a time.
Controls access to limited resources.             Protects a shared resource or critical section.


Conclusion: 
Shared memory and memory-mapped files provide efficient ways for processes to share data. Semaphores and mutexes help synchronize processes and prevent conflicts when accessing shared resources.
Together, these techniques make interprocess communication efficient, controlled, and reliable.
